# EXAMPLE / TEMPLATE — generate the real chain at `static/cert.pem`
# (the real cert.pem is git-ignored so you never commit deployment-specific files).
#
# This is the IDENTITY certificate — the one referenced by `x5u` in did.json and
# used to sign your credentials. It is NOT the web server's TLS certificate.
#
# ── Required structure ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The full chain, in order, leaf first, ENDING IN A SELF-SIGNED ROOT:
#
#   -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
#   <leaf - your organisation's EV SSL certificate>
#   -----END CERTIFICATE-----
#   -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
#   <intermediate CA - repeat for every intermediate, including cross-signed ones>
#   -----END CERTIFICATE-----
#   -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
#   <root CA - subject == issuer>
#   -----END CERTIFICATE-----
#
# Two verified failure modes (tested against the live GAIA-X Registry, 2026-09-15):
#   * root omitted or not self-signed  -> 409 "Root certificate is not self-signed"
#   * a cross-signed intermediate skipped -> 409 "... invalid signature"
#
# Note that `openssl pkcs12 -chain` and certbot's fullchain.pem both usually STOP
# BEFORE the self-signed root — you must append it yourself.
#
# ── How to build it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# From a PKCS#12 (.p12 / .pfx) delivered by your CA:
#   openssl pkcs12 -in your-cert.p12 -nokeys -chain -out static/cert.pem
#   cat root-ca.pem >> static/cert.pem        # the CA's self-signed root
#
# From certbot (lab/dev only — Let's Encrypt is rejected in production):
#   cp /etc/letsencrypt/live/YOUR_DOMAIN/fullchain.pem static/cert.pem
#   curl -s https://letsencrypt.org/certs/isrgrootx1.pem >> static/cert.pem
#
# ── Verify before going further ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The last certificate printed must have subject == issuer:
#   openssl crl2pkcs7 -nocrl -certfile static/cert.pem | openssl pkcs7 -print_certs -noout
#
# scripts/generate_did_json.py checks this automatically and also warns if the leaf
# lacks the EV SSL policy OID 2.23.140.1.1, which every production GXDCH requires.
#
# Then ask the GXDCH registry directly (expect exactly {"result":true}):
#   curl -X POST https://<operator-registry>/v2/api/trustAnchor/chain/file \
#        -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
#        -d '{"uri":"https://YOUR_DOMAIN/cert.pem"}'
#
# See gaia-x-onboarding.md Step 1 for which certificate to buy.
